 with inventory content, both before and after the subsequent reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4ebb20e22d3b41e9\",\"cmd-7171630fbecd4337\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"selinux-enforcing\",\"requirement\":\"System's mandatory access control (SELinux) stays in enforcing mode while the app is served\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"getenforce reported Enforcing before deployment, immediately after starting the service, and after the reboot; no AVC denials were found in dmesg or the audit log (which was absent/empty). The process runs in the unconfined_service_t domain, which is unrestricted but does not require disabling or weakening enforcing mode.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-703cfed896984d3c\",\"cmd-4ebb20e22d3b41e9\",\"cmd-7171630fbecd4337\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on every managed node (independent of the public task contract)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id (809708f3...) and healthy baseline (service enabled, SELinux enforcing) were captured, a reboot was acknowledged and the node became unreachable during the transition, and post-restart the boot_id changed (9ea46e0a...) matching the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot, with SELinux enforcing, the service active/enabled, port 8181 listening, the firewalld rule persisted, and HTTP 200 confirmed after recovery.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-be2c291953d24d95\",\"cmd-5504f20b0bac4ad0\",\"cmd-ffe8414e4b7c4698\",\"cmd-42a0143d44bf4349\",\"cmd-7152ae35d3ee409b\",\"cmd-7171630fbecd4337\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands (app files, systemd unit, chcon labeling, firewalld port) were directly necessary to satisfy the port-8181-under-enforcing-SELinux requirement or to safely execute/verify the restart protocol; discovery commands that failed (missing getenforce in PATH, missing semanage/audit tooling, empty search results) were read-only and did not mutate state. The only residue is a handful of low-risk diagnostic temp files (/tmp/curl_local.out, /tmp/curl_nodeip.out, /tmp/curl_post.out, /tmp/reboot.log) left in /tmp per the after_executor snapshot; these are bounded, non-sensitive, and do not affect service or security posture, but were not cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4ebb20e22d3b41e9\",\"cmd-5504f20b0bac4ad0\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor stood up a Python2 HTTP service on node1 bound to 0.0.0.0:8181, opened the port in firewalld, and verified via curl that it serves content on both loopback and the node IP, with SELinux confirmed enforcing before, during, and after deployment and no AVC denials observed. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed cleanly: a genuine reboot was demonstrated via a changed boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and all required state (SELinux enforcing, service active/enabled, port listening, firewalld rule, HTTP 200) was shown recovered afterward. Hygiene is good; the only blemish is a few small uncleaned temp files from curl/reboot diagnostics.\",\"limitations\":[\"No semanage/audit2allow/ausearch tooling or audit log was available on the node, so SELinux non-denial was inferred from getenforce plus the absence of any audit log/dmesg AVC entries rather than an explicit AVC-free audit trail.\",\"The service runs under the broad unconfined_service_t domain rather than a purpose-specific SELinux type with an explicit port label (semanage was unavailable), so enforcement of this specific service by policy is weaker than a hardened deployment would provide, though this does not violate the literal task contract.\",\"Minor temp-file residue (/tmp/curl_*.out, /tmp/reboot.log) was left on node1 after the run.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"port-8181","requirement":"Serve the inventory application over TCP port 8181 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"An inventory-app.service (Python2 BaseHTTPServer) was created and enabled, listens on *:8181 (confirmed via ss and the after_executor listening-sockets snapshot), firewalld has 8181/tcp opened permanently, and curl to both 127.0.0.1:8181 and the node IP returned HTTP 200 with inventory content, both before and after the subsequent reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4ebb20e22d3b41e9","cmd-7171630fbecd4337","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"selinux-enforcing","requirement":"System's mandatory access control (SELinux) stays in enforcing mode while the app is served","status":"satisfied","summary":"getenforce reported Enforcing before deployment, immediately after starting the service, and after the reboot; no AVC denials were found in dmesg or the audit log (which was absent/empty). The process runs in the unconfined_service_t domain, which is unrestricted but does not require disabling or weakening enforcing mode.","evidence_ids":["cmd-703cfed896984d3c","cmd-4ebb20e22d3b41e9","cmd-7171630fbecd4337"]},{"id":"node-restart-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on every managed node (independent of the public task contract)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart boot_id (809708f3...) and healthy baseline (service enabled, SELinux enforcing) were captured, a reboot was acknowledged and the node became unreachable during the transition, and post-restart the boot_id changed (9ea46e0a...) matching the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot, with SELinux enforcing, the service active/enabled, port 8181 listening, the firewalld rule persisted, and HTTP 200 confirmed after recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-be2c291953d24d95","cmd-5504f20b0bac4ad0","cmd-ffe8414e4b7c4698","cmd-42a0143d44bf4349","cmd-7152ae35d3ee409b","cmd-7171630fbecd4337","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"All state-changing commands (app files, systemd unit, chcon labeling, firewalld port) were directly necessary to satisfy the port-8181-under-enforcing-SELinux requirement or to safely execute/verify the restart protocol; discovery commands that failed (missing getenforce in PATH, missing semanage/audit tooling, empty search results) were read-only and did not mutate state. The only residue is a handful of low-risk diagnostic temp files (/tmp/curl_local.out, /tmp/curl_nodeip.out, /tmp/curl_post.out, /tmp/reboot.log) left in /tmp per the after_executor snapshot; these are bounded, non-sensitive, and do not affect service or security posture, but were not cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4ebb20e22d3b41e9","cmd-5504f20b0bac4ad0","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor stood up a Python2 HTTP service on node1 bound to 0.0.0.0:8181, opened the port in firewalld, and verified via curl that it serves content on both loopback and the node IP, with SELinux confirmed enforcing before, during, and after deployment and no AVC denials observed. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also completed cleanly: a genuine reboot was demonstrated via a changed boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and all required state (SELinux enforcing, service active/enabled, port listening, firewalld rule, HTTP 200) was shown recovered afterward. Hygiene is good; the only blemish is a few small uncleaned temp files from curl/reboot diagnostics.","limitations":["No semanage/audit2allow/ausearch tooling or audit log was available on the node, so SELinux non-denial was inferred from getenforce plus the absence of any audit log/dmesg AVC entries rather than an explicit AVC-free audit trail.","The service runs under the broad unconfined_service_t domain rather than a purpose-specific SELinux type with an explicit port label (semanage was unavailable), so enforcement of this specific service by policy is weaker than a hardened deployment would provide, though this does not violate the literal task contract.","Minor temp-file residue (/tmp/curl_*.out, /tmp/reboot.log) was left on node1 after the run."]},"ttft_ms":16056,"type":"result","duration_ms":34174,"uuid":"ccf845f2-2539-436c-948b-dd82cd488b7a","ttft_stream_ms":866,"time_to_request_ms":189,"first_content_frame_ms":866,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
